rement of -1, the loop counts down from 5 to 1, printing each value and stopping when it goes below 1, giving \"5\\n4\\n3\\n2\\n1\\n\" ✓\n\nFor `seq 5 1 1` where the first value exceeds the last with a positive step, I need to check if the loop even enters.\n\nVerifying the edge cases and test cases...\n\nNow I need to handle the parsing carefully, especially with negative numbers—when I see an argument like \"-1\", I need to distinguish between a negative number operand and a flag prefix.\n\nLet me verify the logic works for sequences with all negative values too. If I run `seq -5 -1 -10`, the parser correctly identifies all three as operands, then the loop counts down from -5 to -10 in steps of -1, printing each value until it goes below the endpoint.\n\nNow I'm defining the `isNumStart` helper function to distinguish numeric arguments from option flags—it checks if the second character is either a digit or decimal point, which correctly handles cases like \"-5\", \"-.5\", and \"-w\".\n\nI need to verify the edge cases: a bare \"-\" has length 1 so it won't match the flag pattern and gets treated as a regular operand, which is appropriate since `parseNumber` will reject it anyway. I should also confirm that I need to import `math` for the `IsNaN` and `IsInf` checks in the number parsing logic, and I'm realizing that Go's `strconv.ParseFloat` doesn't actually return NaN for invalid inputs—it returns an error instead. I'm realizing that treating `-inf` as a flag parse error versus a numeric argument error creates a discrepancy with GNU seq's behavior, but since the non-goals explicitly exclude infinity support, keeping the simpler implementation that errors on `-inf` as an invalid option seems reasonable for now—the exit code matches even if the error message differs slightly.\n\nFor handling positive prefixes like `+5`, the parser already works correctly since `ParseFloat` accepts the `+` sign and my logic routes non-dash arguments directly to the operand case.\n\nNow I'm verifying the formatting behavior—`%.0f` produces clean integers while preserving decimal precision for float sequences, so the output formatting should work as expected.\n\nI'm not implementing the `-w` flag behavior yet since that's a later requirement, but I'll parse it to avoid errors. The default format won't consider equal-width alignment, which is fine for this task.\n\nWriting the implementation... Setting up the signal handler, then parsing command-line arguments and operands to extract the sequence parameters, handling any errors that come up along the way. Once I have the valid inputs, I'll generate and print the sequence using the specified options. Writing the flag parsing logic... Writing the flag parser... Continuing with the operand parsing logic...\n\nNow I'm finishing the number parsing function by validating that the result isn't NaN or infinite, then moving into the sequence printing logic where I set up buffered output and determine the format to use, either from options or by inferring it from the raw arguments.\n\nContinuing with the sequence generation... Continuing to verify the function sizes, I'm checking parseArgs more carefully by counting from the opening brace through the return statement, and it does come in at 33 lines. Looking at the remaining functions—parseNumber, printSequence, inRange, defaultFormat, and argPrecision—they're all well under the 40-line limit, ranging from 6 to 19 lines each. Removing blank lines to stay within the function size limit...\n\nThe rest of the function handles the different argument types—long flags starting with `--`, short flags starting with `-`, and operands. It collects non-flag arguments, then validates that we have between 1 and 3 operands, returning an error if there are too many or too few.\n\nNow I'm checking the `parseLongFlag` function to see how it handles the various command-line options like `--help`, `--version`, and `--format`...\n\nContinuing with `parseShortFlags`... Finishing the switch statement and returning the consumed count. Now I'm checking the line count—we're at 35 lines, which is under the 40-line limit. Before finalizing, I want to make sure the `parseOperands` function handles edge cases properly, particularly around argument validation and panic prevention when accessing array indices. I need to verify that the `cmd/seq/` directory exists before writing the file, and I should also think through what happens if the increment is zero—the loop could become infinite if that edge case isn't handled properly. After parseOperands returns, I need to check if the increment is zero and exit with an error if it is. I'm adding this validation to the main function right after parsing the operands.\n\nNow I'm realizing there's an issue with the error message—when there's only one operand, there's no operands[1] to reference, so I need to guard against that and use a default value for the error message. I'm realizing the error handling in main() is getting too verbose with all these repeated fprintf calls. Let me think about how many lines this is taking up and whether I should refactor it into a helper function to keep main() cleaner. I'm refactoring the error handling by extracting a helper function that consolidates the repeated error messages and exit logic, then updating main to use it for cleaner code.\n\nNow I need to verify the complete imports list and write out the final file with all the necessary packages for the bufio writer, float parsing, string utilities, and the SIGPIPE handler.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15458,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15458},"output_tokens":36,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41be0d6d-a54d-41f0-8d12-7cbd962